WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.493 --> 00:00:08.483
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:55.427 --> 00:09:00.417
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:06.890
Good evening.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:11.740
Welcome to this special concert
in honor of Puerto Rico.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:17.890
And you just heard the fantastic
amazing son of Puerto Rico,

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:18.700
Miguel Zenón--

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:24.150
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.480
--and an array of soloists,
Ini Oguntola on the clarinet--

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:33.130
--Tony Terrasa on
the alto saxophone--

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.940
--Larry Wang and Peter
Goddard on piano--

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:39.980
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.370
--Dylan Sleeper on
tenor saxophone--

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.557
--Alan Osmundson
on the trumpet--

00:09:46.557 --> 00:09:47.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:50.840
--and Chase [INAUDIBLE]
on the drums.

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.040
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:01.097
This is an original composition
by Miguel entitled Ceremonial.

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:06.087
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:07.022 --> 00:23:10.515
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:13.509 --> 00:23:17.090
Alan Osmundson on
the flugelhorn.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:23.310
Miguel Zenón--
composer, saxophonist.

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:33.990
Part of this journey that
we're on-- as you may know,

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:37.890
this ensemble along with Miguel
Zenón and my amazing colleague

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:40.350
Evan Ziporyn, who I will
properly introduce right now--

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:43.230
Evan Ziporyn, bass
clarinet, other clarinets.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:45.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:50.940
We were all slated to go to
Puerto Rico this past January.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:52.500
And that wasn't possible.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.870
But we are going January of '19.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:56.066
We're going to be there.

00:23:56.066 --> 00:23:57.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:57.730 --> 00:24:03.360
And part of this journey was
an education for me, not only

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:07.570
about the plight of the
island, past and present,

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:09.570
but also getting to know
what's been going on

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:13.050
in our MIT community in
terms of relief and support

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:13.890
for Puerto Rico.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:15.390
So it's been a great
pleasure to get

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:18.900
to know the students of the
MIT Association of Puerto Rican

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:20.350
Students here at MIT.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:23.370
So I want to introduce you
to the president and the vice

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:24.120
president.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:30.570
This is Gabriel Ginorio
and Nina Fraticelli-Guzmán.

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:31.860
Please welcome them.

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:39.540
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:40.673
Hello, everyone.

00:24:40.673 --> 00:24:42.840
I hope you're feeling nice
and dry and warm in here.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:44.670
I know it's a really cold night.

00:24:44.670 --> 00:24:47.280
But it's never a bad night
to hear some good music.

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.080
Hi, I'm Nina Fraticelli.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.570
And I am vice president of the
Association of Puerto Rican

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:52.290
Students.

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:53.730
And I'm Gabriel Ginorio.

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.490
I'm president of the MIT
Association of Puerto Rican

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:57.270
Students.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:01.230
And we first want to thank all
the MIT festival jazz ensemble

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:05.100
as well as Miguel Zenón for
coming together and making this

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.048
concert.

00:25:06.048 --> 00:25:08.340
We want to thank you all for
sharing your music with us

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:10.440
as well as for choosing
to have your music be

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:14.355
a means of bringing
support to the island

00:25:14.355 --> 00:25:17.580
as well as raising awareness
to its current state.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:20.880
Last semester, once
Hurricane Maria hit home,

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.760
APR was hard at
work to do our best

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:27.030
to raise awareness and
funds to help our island.

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.270
And we began by joining forces
with different universities

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:33.960
across the nation and started a
GoFundMe campaign that ended up

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:37.170
raising around $250,000.

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:40.200
That money was donated to
United for Puerto Rico,

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:44.280
an organization that helped
bring aid at critical moments

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:47.550
as well as donated money to
nonprofits on the island.

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:50.670
We then proceeded
to work on campus

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:53.910
and started a donation drive
that lasted three days.

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.410
And during those
three days, we were

00:25:55.410 --> 00:25:58.710
able to see a the MIT
community's generosity

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.640
and support for us.

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:03.360
We ended up raising
around $6,000

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:06.570
that we donated to a
nonprofit on the island

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:10.350
and raised around four to six
pallets worth of goods ranging

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:15.600
from animal products to food,
canned food, and hygiene

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:18.930
and personal products.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.980
And after the initial
aid phase, we now--

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:25.320
or some of our students
actually went back

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:29.010
to Puerto Rico in
January to work

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.950
on houses in the rural
part of Puerto Rico

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:35.550
to rebuild the roofs.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.590
There's another group
of Puerto Rican students

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.020
that went to do field
work and research what

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:42.510
the lay of the land
was after the hurricane

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:45.540
to come back and report
to the MIT community

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:48.070
to figure out what
we could do later on.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.910
There were students who
worked in the government

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:54.690
to try to create better digital
systems for the governor

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:59.130
of Puerto Rico to
be faster to respond

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.960
after natural disasters.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.110
And ongoing, there
are various things

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:06.390
that the MIT community,
non-Puerto Ricans,

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:08.430
that our part of our
community are doing,

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:11.250
spring workshops and
classes, and thinking

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:13.650
about research
projects in the island.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:19.410
And to that we helped
create a conference called

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:22.320
MIT Conference on the Resilient
Reconstruction of the Caribbean

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.790
that happened last December.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.220
And we were lucky to
bring the governor

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.860
to actually speak to us and to
talk about what the issues are

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:31.710
on the island regarding
electricity, housing

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:33.480
infrastructure,
and water supply.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:34.980
And people from all
around the world

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.110
and people from
the MIT community

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:38.970
came together to
propose solutions.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:41.340
And hopefully, those
will carry on forward

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.890
with a formal MIT,
Puerto Rico, and

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:45.897
the Caribbean collaboration.

00:27:45.897 --> 00:27:46.980
That's still in the works.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:50.550
And we hope to be able to
announce more developments

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:51.270
to come.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:55.110
The moral of the story here is
that Puerto Rico may be small

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:56.580
and in the middle of the ocean.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:01.630
But we're here to support it
as best we can with our talent.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:05.070
And to the future of Puerto
Rico, I say, bring it on.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:17.570
And again, we want to thank
the ensemble as well as Miguel.

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:20.120
And we want to thank you,
the audience, for supporting.

00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:21.740
And with that, we
want to welcome

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:24.860
to stage the gentleman that
helped made it possible

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:26.960
for us to take all our
goods to the island

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:29.060
and for it to reach the
people that needed it

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:30.710
at the most critical moments.

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:34.003
Please help us in welcoming
Pastor Henry Monroy.

00:28:34.003 --> 00:28:38.933
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:55.620
Hello, everyone.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:58.910
My name is Henry Monroy, a
pastor at Hyssop Christian

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.170
Church in Dorchester.

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:07.855
From the beginning of
this whole mission,

00:29:07.855 --> 00:29:09.480
I was actually trying
to connect myself

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:14.520
with a larger organization.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.280
Some of you guys
might know who he

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.360
is, Kurt Schilling, because
he was promoting that he

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:22.550
was going to help Puerto Rico.

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:26.030
And we started
connecting with him.

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:30.170
But something happened between
the process of sending things

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:32.150
to Puerto Rico.

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:35.180
Nobody was sending
nothing because there

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:36.980
was a miscommunication
or something

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:41.420
was happening within the
island that nothing was moving.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:49.250
So we started raising first aid
from everyone in our church.

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:51.920
I was assuming that I was going
to raise probably two pallets

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:56.930
and probably raise $1,000
and ship it to Puerto Rico.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:30:01.550
Within 24 hours,
our church was half

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:07.340
full of boxes of first aid
items, diapers, and anything

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:10.490
that anyone needed at the time.

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:12.650
That's when I started
realizing that it

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:14.210
was a little bit more serious.

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:16.880
And The Boston Globe
reached out to me.

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:22.810
Once that happened, I just knew
that this was going to go big.

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:24.820
Little by little, people
started continuing

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:26.650
to come to our church.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:32.380
And we had different schools
and organizations reaching out

00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:33.260
to us.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:38.230
And within four to
five days, we had

00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:41.290
to start canceling our
services because there were so

00:30:41.290 --> 00:30:47.350
many cases of water, first
aid items in our facility

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:50.560
that we couldn't
have no more service.

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:53.410
That's when we
connected with MIT.

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:56.890
And it happened
through Facebook.

00:30:56.890 --> 00:31:00.070
And then we came
and we met the group

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:05.670
of students that were outside
collecting other items.

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:07.200
And we just connected.

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:10.040
And I tell them, hey, whatever
it is that you guys need,

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:11.350
I'm here to help out.

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:16.460
And let's just connect
and make this happen.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.850
So shortly after that--

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.220
yeah, that's the
team right there.

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:22.750
We came and pulled
up to our truck.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.010
And we just started
filling all the stuff.

00:31:25.010 --> 00:31:26.500
Our church is very small.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:31.495
And one of our friends, he
came to the church and said,

00:31:31.495 --> 00:31:32.620
hey, you got to be careful.

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:35.260
The structure of the building
might not hold all the stuff

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:37.580
that you have.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:39.503
Yeah, that's when I
called the Boston Police.

00:31:39.503 --> 00:31:41.920
And I asked them if they could
let us borrow their parking

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:45.910
lot because we had over 20
pallets of water, people

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:49.660
calling from New Hampshire,
Maine, all over Massachusetts.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:55.360
And I just thought this was
going to be two pallets.

00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:58.780
Surely, once I started
connecting with people,

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:02.740
U-Hauls were pulling
in front of the church

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.170
just unloading all the
stuff that they had.

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:08.200
And we just said, the doors
of the church is open.

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:10.520
Bring it on.

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:13.970
And surely, it was a lot
of stuff that we had.

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:19.280
But right here, we had Boston
Mothers Care, UMass Lowell,

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.170
Blue Cross Blue Shield.

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:27.230
And all this happened within
hours of just telling people

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.030
to help us.

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:30.620
And just people showed up.

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:32.190
It wasn't just a
Puerto Rico thing.

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:34.273
It wasn't just Puerto
Ricans that were coming out.

00:32:34.273 --> 00:32:35.300
Everyone was coming out.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:37.760
Everyone united, everyone
was helping us financially.

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:39.930
Everyone was helping
with donations.

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:43.970
And this is how things
get done when people unite

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:46.340
as one for the same cause.

00:32:46.340 --> 00:32:48.020
And it's to help humanity out.

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:50.970
Sometimes we need help
from someone else.

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:54.102
That's why it's important to
connect with other people.

00:32:54.102 --> 00:32:55.310
This is one of the gentlemen.

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:57.170
We were filling up the truck.

00:32:57.170 --> 00:33:00.350
On Saturday, October
7, I get a phone

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:02.510
call at 3:00 in the morning.

00:33:02.510 --> 00:33:06.190
So I was a little bit concerned
because there's nothing moving.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:08.170
They're not accepting
nothing in the island.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:11.770
And the boats-- all the
shipping companies are on hold.

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:14.170
At 3:00 in the morning,
I get a phone call

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:17.260
from a gentleman
called Roger Cruz.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.030
He's Cuban.

00:33:19.030 --> 00:33:21.700
And he tells me, Henry, I'm
sorry that I'm calling you

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:23.330
at 3:00 in the morning.

00:33:23.330 --> 00:33:25.630
But I have an opportunity
that opened up.

00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:29.911
And it's an overnight shipping--

00:33:29.911 --> 00:33:31.300
a flight.

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:34.310
And I said, how is
this going to happen?

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:37.750
And he said, we have to drive
from Boston to Orlando, Florida

00:33:37.750 --> 00:33:38.380
right now.

00:33:41.323 --> 00:33:42.990
And I said, how are
we going to do that?

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.470
I have like over 20
pallets of water.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:50.930
I have like 20 pallets of
diapers, 20 pallets of formula,

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:52.730
20 pallets of food.

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.580
And he said, I'm going to
rent a U-Haul in the morning.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.410
And whatever fits
in that U-Haul,

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.090
we're taking it to Orlando.

00:33:59.090 --> 00:34:01.040
And I said, all right.

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:02.780
Let's cancel our Sunday service.

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:08.778
And sure enough, people started
coming out within half an hour

00:34:08.778 --> 00:34:12.139
of letting people know,
hey, we need help.

00:34:12.139 --> 00:34:14.389
In half an hour, we
had a large group

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:17.120
of people just helping us out.

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:20.179
And within the next 24 hours,
Roger Cruz and his girlfriend,

00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:23.360
Mari Gloria, they drove
to Orlando, Florida

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.429
and made it to FedEx.

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:31.040
The FAA opened up
a door of 50 tons,

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.469
a flight that would
take 24 hours basically

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:35.370
for it to get to Puerto Rico.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:40.363
So the next day, I'm getting
a ticket to go to Puerto Rico.

00:34:40.363 --> 00:34:41.780
And I didn't know
that I was going

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:45.530
to go to Puerto Rico
that next following day.

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:48.500
Once I get to Puerto Rico--
that's me in Carolina,

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:52.850
Puerto Rico with all that
stuff within 24 hours.

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:54.350
And the best thing
about this was

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:55.999
that it was completely free.

00:34:55.999 --> 00:34:57.193
We didn't pay a dollar.

00:34:57.193 --> 00:34:58.610
And at the end of
the whole thing,

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:02.480
we sent 27 pallets
without paying a dime.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:05.780
And thanks to the
FAA that allowed us

00:35:05.780 --> 00:35:07.700
to send all these things over.

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:10.130
This is a part of
the group that went

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.560
to the south side
of Puerto Rico.

00:35:12.560 --> 00:35:17.570
They took 12 pallets of first
aid items to help them out.

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:22.400
This is pallets of baby
formula and diapers and stuff

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:27.240
like that that we had sent
down there to Puerto Rico.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:29.330
So this is the Bank
Oriental that's

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:31.760
in San Juan, Puerto Rico.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:35.150
I connected with a
gentleman Rauli Santos.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:40.100
And within three days, we
asked Oriental, the bank,

00:35:40.100 --> 00:35:41.210
to give us a space.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:43.820
And they said, take
the whole first floor

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:45.950
and just put all the stuff
there and get organized.

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:47.820
So that's exactly what we did.

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:54.840
We started getting organized and
preparing packages for family.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:57.810
We had over 300
packages of family

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:01.160
to help them for the
next four or five days.

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:04.160
This is the group that
Rauli Santos connected us

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:07.640
with in Puerto Rico.

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:10.400
The company that
they were working for

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:14.360
told them that they can work
four hours of community service

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:16.440
and four hours of
just regular work.

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:18.410
So we just took
advantage of the time.

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:22.010
And we just started unloading
and packing up all the stuff.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:24.920
And this is us getting prepared
to go to the western side

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:28.260
of Puerto Rico, a good team.

00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:34.490
And before we left, we prayed
and asked God for guidance

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:36.600
for this mission.

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:39.040
Once we arrived to the
western side of Puerto Rico--

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:40.605
so I just want to pause.

00:36:40.605 --> 00:36:45.520
Mind you, I didn't
plan none of this.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:47.330
I'm just in it for the ride.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:51.310
So everything-- it
was like a puzzle.

00:36:51.310 --> 00:36:54.280
And everything felt like
it went-- everything just

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:55.510
fell into place.

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:58.540
And it was just amazing.

00:36:58.540 --> 00:37:00.670
These two gentlemen
are veterans.

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:03.063
They came from Texas.

00:37:03.063 --> 00:37:04.480
They're not getting
paid for this.

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.640
They're doing this
absolutely volunteer.

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:08.710
And they're just
protecting us from anyone

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:15.370
that tried to step out
of their line I will say.

00:37:15.370 --> 00:37:18.250
In the western side of Puerto
Rico, there was no water.

00:37:18.250 --> 00:37:21.650
Like legitimately,
there's no water at all.

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:24.160
And we found this--

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:27.370
we found this lady
with her newborn baby.

00:37:27.370 --> 00:37:30.190
The baby was born exactly
the same day the storm hit.

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:31.540
And she had no formula.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:33.610
And there was no water.

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:36.770
Without water, you can't
mix up the formula.

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:41.920
So we made sure that she
had enough food and items

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:44.230
for the baby for the next week.

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:49.090
This gentleman, we found him
in Mayagüez, Puerto Rico.

00:37:49.090 --> 00:37:53.290
Before the storm,
he had diabetes.

00:37:53.290 --> 00:37:56.050
And his legs were amputated.

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:59.740
And he rode the storm
basically in his house.

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:02.080
The water was six feet high.

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:06.180
And his son was scraping
mud off the walls

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:14.790
while we was attending him with
veterans from Texas that also

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:16.260
joined us to help them out.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:19.760
This gentleman, we also
met him in Mayagüez.

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:24.600
And he was cleaning out his car
because all the river came out.

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:27.633
And she was trying
to clean out his car

00:38:27.633 --> 00:38:28.800
to make sure that it worked.

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:35.220
But he wouldn't be able to
move his car due to the flood.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:36.990
The lady that we
helped out as well with

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:40.080
formula and water
for the babies.

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.720
I guess that was
number 1 thing for us

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:46.500
to make sure that these babies
were drinking clean water

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:47.510
and food.

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:53.850
In the south side
of Puerto Rico,

00:38:53.850 --> 00:38:58.510
our group met with this lady.

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:02.920
She started crying because
nobody has helped her out

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:04.300
since the storm hit.

00:39:04.300 --> 00:39:08.970
And this was one of
the pictures that

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:13.050
pushed me and made me realize
that we was doing a great job.

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:14.925
She was crying because
she hasn't drank water

00:39:14.925 --> 00:39:16.560
in over 14 days.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:21.040
And we attended her.

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:23.530
We helped the whole community
from the south side.

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:26.390
And at the end of the day, she
hugged everyone from the team

00:39:26.390 --> 00:39:31.480
and was very happy and
excited that we weren't there.

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:34.140
So while this is all
happening, I just

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:38.790
want to inform that, in San
Juan, most of the people in San

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:41.580
Juan, well, they were
all right in a sense

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:45.490
that there was some water
but it was very limited.

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:50.580
Then we had to drive
2 hours and 45 minutes

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:55.875
to help those people that
didn't have that help.

00:39:55.875 --> 00:40:02.070
This is the military people from
Texas that was helping us out--

00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:03.510
great guys.

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:07.200
They helped us out very nice.

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:07.920
The sign says,

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:10.260
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:17.490
So basically, what it
said is, "Puerto Rico

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:21.120
will rise without
lights, without water,

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:23.430
or without communication."

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:26.800
That was a very powerful
statement because, at the time,

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:31.680
there wasn't a base where the
United States military was

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:35.220
taking out water and purifying
the water just for people to be

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:40.740
able to take a shower and
wash their clothes and dishes

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:41.600
and stuff like that.

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:48.621
So this is a military
spot as well.

00:40:48.621 --> 00:40:50.810
This was in the--

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:52.910
what you see right
here in the side

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:56.420
where the two military
guys are freezers.

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:01.880
The death toll of
Puerto Rico, there

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.715
was over 1,000 people
that died in Puerto Rico.

00:41:04.715 --> 00:41:09.890
So the building itself couldn't
hold all the bodies inside.

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:15.160
So the military had to
come out and place freezers

00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:18.220
outside for the bodies.

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:21.950
So all these images that
I try to present here

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:25.450
is just to show
that it wasn't just

00:41:25.450 --> 00:41:28.800
something that just came and
just knocked out water light.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.480
People actually-- their
lives were really affected.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:36.230
And a lot of people lost their
lives in this tragic event.

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:46.580
So I went to Puerto
Rico, a one-way trip.

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:51.140
And when our mission finished, I
said, let me go back to Boston.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:52.510
I called JetBlue.

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:54.260
And they told me,
there's no more flights.

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:56.690
So I started reaching out
to every other airline.

00:41:56.690 --> 00:41:59.450
And they're telling me, there's
no more flights till November.

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:03.012
So now I'm stuck in Puerto Rico.

00:42:03.012 --> 00:42:04.220
I'm stuck in Puerto Rico now.

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:05.887
And I have nothing
to do because we just

00:42:05.887 --> 00:42:07.430
finished giving everything out.

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:10.610
And I get a phone call
from someone on Facebook

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:14.390
saying that there's a doctor
that's coming from California.

00:42:14.390 --> 00:42:15.890
At the time when
the storm happened,

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:18.530
there was a wildfire that
was happening in California.

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:24.137
And Dr. Lamaris and
Leslie reached out to us.

00:42:24.137 --> 00:42:26.720
And they said, we have two plane
tickets to go to Puerto Rico.

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:28.217
We need your help.

00:42:28.217 --> 00:42:29.300
I don't know who they are.

00:42:29.300 --> 00:42:31.940
I said, sure come on.

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:37.600
And they came loaded
with medication,

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.980
things to help people out, their
diabetes, high blood pressure.

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:43.570
All this medication
they came with.

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:45.490
And we just started
helping people out.

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:47.500
We started going to
supermarkets in San Juan

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:50.530
and just getting
gallons of water

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:52.840
to take it to the western
side of Puerto Rico

00:42:52.840 --> 00:42:54.340
to help more people out.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:57.910
And that's us just going
door by door, reaching out

00:42:57.910 --> 00:43:01.930
to those that can't
move, they can't

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:07.450
go to the hospital or the
clinics within the island.

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:10.090
That's me in Loiza,
Puerto Rico with the kids.

00:43:13.730 --> 00:43:15.920
There's a lady in Loiza,
Puerto Rico as well.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:17.570
She lost her whole house.

00:43:17.570 --> 00:43:20.142
And yet she's still
living within the house.

00:43:20.142 --> 00:43:21.100
There's no electricity.

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:22.220
There's no water.

00:43:22.220 --> 00:43:24.560
And her structure is clearly--

00:43:24.560 --> 00:43:26.000
the house was not livable.

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:33.400
This picture, it was
very impacting to me

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:35.590
because all you see is
devastation all over.

00:43:35.590 --> 00:43:42.910
But then you see a little kid
swinging or in a playground.

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:45.250
That, for me, right there
represents Puerto Rico.

00:43:45.250 --> 00:43:47.800
In the midst of a
disaster, how can you

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:50.380
still focus and have fun.

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:52.233
And that's what this
little boy was doing.

00:43:52.233 --> 00:43:54.400
And that's why I had to
snap this picture because it

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:57.970
was so impacting that
I had to make sure

00:43:57.970 --> 00:43:59.240
that I take it in image.

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:02.470
These are some of the doctors
as well that basically helped us

00:44:02.470 --> 00:44:05.800
out to reach the certain
pueblos and make sure

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:10.920
that everybody was attended
with medication and so forth.

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:16.770
That's a group that went also
to the western side of Puerto

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:20.370
Rico with food and items
to help out others.

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:28.450
And that's another group
that we met in Puerto Rico.

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:31.780
And we went to the south side
of Puerto Rico on this occasion

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:35.920
to help out a school that
their whole structure was

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:37.240
gone in this.

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:52.570
So we went to Puerto
Rico in October.

00:44:52.570 --> 00:44:54.840
We came back in December.

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:57.660
And we left-- in
November, I apologize.

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.450
And then went back in December
again to help out Puerto Rico.

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:02.040
We ended up sending
to Puerto Rico

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:05.580
three containers filled
with first aid items.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:09.000
And it helped out
a lot of people.

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:10.620
A few thousand
people were helped

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:14.730
through the help of everyone
here in Boston, everyone

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:16.480
in the state of Massachusetts.

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:20.850
And all this could not be
done if we did not unite.

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:22.133
And I'm thankful for you guys.

00:45:22.133 --> 00:45:23.550
And thank you for
this opportunity

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:25.500
you guys have given me.

00:45:25.500 --> 00:45:27.190
If this was to happen--

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:29.010
I told this to the professor.

00:45:29.010 --> 00:45:32.340
I feel like I'm in debt
with a lot of people

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:36.010
because everybody helped my
people out when they needed it.

00:45:36.010 --> 00:45:39.900
So thank you so much,
guys, for this opportunity.

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:42.310
And I appreciate all you guys.

00:45:42.310 --> 00:45:42.810
Thank you.

00:45:42.810 --> 00:45:47.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:58.020
Thank you, Henry,
for all you've done

00:45:58.020 --> 00:45:59.220
and all you continue to do.

